"Let $(\\Gamma,+)$ be a finite abelian group, and let $\\mathcal{G}$ be the set of finite abelian groups that either have odd order or contain more than one involution. For integers $m,n>1$ and $c\\geq 1$, an $\\mathrm{MRS}_\\Gamma(m,n;c)$ is a set of $c$ $m\\times n$ arrays whose entries are the elements of $\\Gamma$, each appearing once, with constant row sums and constant column sums in every array. **Cichacz–Hinc conjecture.** An $\\mathrm{MRS}_\\Gamma(m,n;c)$ exists if and only if $m$ and $n$ are both even, or $\\Gamma\\in\\mathcal{G}$ and $\\{m,n\\}\\neq\\{2\\ell+1,2\\}$. The construction of magic rectangle sets is generally open, although necessary and sufficient conditions for their existence are known; this conjecture proposes a complete characterization in terms of the parity of $m,n$ and the structure of $\\Gamma$."